<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Parthenopean Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1799 CE – 1799 CE · capital Naples</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Neapolitan republic of 1799, whose idealistic leaders were crushed by Cardinal Ruffo's peasant army and executed in their hundreds.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples</a> attacked the French in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in late 1798 and was defeated, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">King Ferdinand IV</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. French troops under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-etienne-championnet">Championnet</a> fought their way into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and in January 1799 local patriots proclaimed a republic named after <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/neapolis">Parthenope</a>, the ancient Greek name for the city.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic was led by intellectuals and liberal nobles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-lauberg">Carlo Lauberg</a> and the jurist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-mario-pagano">Francesco Mario Pagano</a>, who drafted a constitution. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleonora-fonseca-pimentel">Eleonora Fonseca Pimentel</a> edited its newspaper, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monitore-napoletano">Monitore Napoletano</a>. It tried to abolish feudalism, but had little support among the poor and peasants, and depended on French troops, who withdrew in May.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fabrizio-ruffo">Fabrizio Ruffo's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/army-of-the-holy-faith">Sanfedisti</a> army retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in June 1799. The surrender terms were broken with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Nelson</a>'s backing, and over a hundred republicans, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-mario-pagano">Pagano</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleonora-fonseca-pimentel">Pimentel</a> and Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-caracciolo">Francesco Caracciolo</a>, were executed.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/parthenopean-republic.jpg" alt="Parthenopean Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Parthenopaean_Republic.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1799 CE – 1799 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Naples</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Carlo Lauberg, Francesco Mario Pagano</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Neapolitan, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Neapolitan Republic (1799), Repubblica Partenopea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1799&amp;focus=parthenopean-republic" aria-label="Parthenopean Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5029 720 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Parthenopean Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5349 976L5349 976M5349 976L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5349 976h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5349 976h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5349 976h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Naples. <a href="/atlas?year=1799&amp;focus=parthenopean-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
